Notebook[{
Cell["Hypermatrix", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"03f9c81e-91e8-ca47-94ba-953b8c030165"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "a7c68134-cae1-4f47-9789-c7ece04619e7"],
 " ",
 "represents the given arrays as a hypermatrix, holding them in canonical \
order."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"6fbbc946-acef-574d-b74d-92cefe0de8ef"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ae9cfa46-ee6f-c845-8ae1-168dcda59ba7"],
 " ",
 "is equivalent."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"18622ff2-53a9-ff47-bc73-910b97c8e736"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"157baa7a-1047-5a4c-ad2e-21391772f448"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays are ordered by array order, lowest first; then by dimensions, \
shorter first, compared term by term; then by symmetry, taking \"Symmetric\" \
before \"Cyclic\" before \"Rigid\".\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a0205eac-2a7d-bc4f-a9fe-75603ea6f8b9"],

Cell["\<\
Two arrays alike in all three are put in the Wolfram Language's own canonical \
order, so that a hypermatrix is a function of the set of arrays it was given \
and of nothing else. Giving the same arrays in a different order produces the \
identical expression.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"fb903363-ab39-d942-bed5-8fd4d9b587ac"],

Cell["\<\
An irregular array is necessarily \"Rigid\", since only a regular array can \
carry a symmetry, so the symmetry criterion separates the regular arrays \
among themselves.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a63d5a7a-c595-8c4c-ae47-eec9ade76e8e"],

Cell["\<\
Arrays of any kind of entry sit together freely. Raw entries given in place \
of an array are promoted to an ArrayObject.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"dd8f3c8b-fa91-044b-8df8-506b4570d8fc"],

Cell["\<\
Length gives the number of arrays, and Normal the entries of each of them in \
order.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f13366d8-7809-5f4e-9427-ea43d367aa77"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"57061855-ad88-ee4e-bc41-eb37eb393867"],

Cell["The arrays are sorted whatever order they are given in:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"bfce5add-2a01-7941-ae18-53049b3deee5"],

Cell["\<\
hm = Hypermatrix[{
   ArrayObject[Array[1 &, {2, 2, 2}], \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2, 3}],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Cyclic\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e4045c73-df87-dd4f-848c-47fb53fae3cd"],

